<id/05/0572/#concept> a <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E55_Type>, skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Whalebone"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "baleen"@en ;
    skos:broader <id/sub/sub_fertilizer_wd_q275426/#concept> ;
    skos:closeMatch <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300192974>, <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q275426> ;
    skos:definition "the commercial name for the baleen plates in the mouth of the whale, of which there are about 300. They are chiefly obtained from the Greenland and Southern whales, *Balaena mysticetus*, and *australis*. It is softened by boiling, and is dyed black. The principal use of whalebone is for the ribs or stretchers for umbrellas, for canes, whips, and as a substitute for bristles in common brushes. In the form of shavings, it is sometimes braided into hats and bonnets. See **FIXNER-WHALE**. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 422]"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Curated classification (land_basis=marine): Marine origin (whalebone, shells, sea otter, marine-animal and fish skins). Layer-1 observations: 63 rows, 1886–1906. Series: HCPP_Annual Statement of Trade ×63. Units: CWTS (Hundredweights) ×63. Top routes: France → United Kingdom ×5; World → United Kingdom ×5; Belgium → United Kingdom ×5; Other British Possessions → United Kingdom ×5."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <id/scheme/#resource> ;
